  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific oral AUC, Cmax, or IV-comparator PK data are available.",
    "No query-specific aqueous solubility, dissolution, or permeability data are available.",
    "No query-specific first-pass metabolism, CYP, or transporter data are available.",
    "L4 records are solubility/dissolution measurements for analog molecules, not direct F measurements or query-specific absorption data.",
    "The effects of the pyridine N-oxide, chloroquinoline, trifluoromethyl, and aminopyrido[3,2-d]pyrimidine on absorption and first-pass metabolism are not directly evidenced."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high because the most transferable human absolute-bioavailability contexts (vismodegib 31.8%, dasatinib 20–34%, erlotinib ~59%, bosentan ~50%, sildenafil ~41%, celecoxib ~0.72) support F≥20% for lipophilic, poorly soluble heteroaromatic drugs; L3 oral exposure records confirm measurable systemic exposure after oral dosing, and L4 solubility records for erlotinib/vismodegib show that poor solubility alone does not establish F<20%. Low non-human and structurally dissimilar low-F values are species/formulation-specific and do not affirmatively establish F<20%."
}

